Z||000007||fierospeeder||03-29-2000||03:22 AM||||Its like a 50/50 thing.  I do own a beetle, and i knew 3 years before the new beetle came out that the idea existed.  If they do reintroduce the fiero to the public, it might sell like the new beetle.  The old beetles were junk to most people, complaints of no heat, rust, bad handling, slow.....But people still loved them.  The sales for the new beetle were high, unless its just yuppies buying them to seem trendy.  I might buy one later in life as a collector car.  If the fiero is reintroduced, it might be a totally different car, just like the new beetle is.   The new beetle lacks an engine in the rear, and rear wheel drive.  Will the new fiero face the same thing?  Will the people buying the new fiero think they are better then the people who originally owned fieros?  It wont be the same car, and it wont have the same experiences.  What would be kewl if GM made the same body as the 88 and just put 3800s in them, and a convertible model.  In reality they would have to redesigned the car for airbags.  The car might be just like a new beetle and all you can do is add a spoiler and graphite dash panel.  It would be different if the fiero evolved to the year 2000, but its being invented over again and it might suck.  Seeing someone in a 2000 fiero with a 5 inch exhaust reving their engine to race you while your in your original fiero saying to yourself, i should have put more stickers on it.  <BR>There are many cons and pros in something like this issue.  If this does happen, we might find used fiero prices rising, i seen vw bugs for more then 5000, while blue book values are less then 3000?  It would be neat to see a prototype but it will never be the same car.    <BR>||||reg||1
